{
  "dom_id": "step-39884",
  "name": "A Faster Way of Computing a Product Using Models",
  "problem": "There's another way we can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the multiplication problem below:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10} \\times 7\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model to represent $\\dfrac{1}{10}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14129500/assets/62b50e658ed9bc691a9559548d09f87fbd3b33e007e81b5e78a3235ac4bc3280.png)\n\n \n\nNow, rather than repeating our model $7$ times (which would take a long time), we can instead multiply the number of shaded pieces by $7.$ \n\nOur model has $1$ shaded piece. So, the resulting model must have $1 \\times 7 = 7$ shaded pieces: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14129500/assets/2a7d54b1d7a6a6013ee64b601d9dc0ba31c8a25dae0f5f69abba4b0fed0e8ee2.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\\times 7 = \\dfrac{7}{10}.\n$$",
